WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.081
Can an image generator learn to draw how far away everything is,

00:00:04.081 --> 00:00:05.573
with almost no new parts?

00:00:09.502 --> 00:00:10.570
Hi, I'm Perpetua!

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.061
This is Iris-Z, a fine-tune of FLUX.2.

00:00:13.061 --> 00:00:15.197
Prompt in, picture and depth out.

00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:16.621
Photo in, depth out.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:19.104
Chapter one.

00:00:19.104 --> 00:00:20.490
Two ways to get depth.

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:26.939
Here is the task.

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.484
A photograph dissolves into its depth map: red is near, blue is

00:00:30.484 --> 00:00:31.960
far, the sky is black.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.676
Specialists are networks built for this one job, and the current leader

00:00:36.676 --> 00:00:37.768
is one of them.

00:00:38.188 --> 00:00:43.354
Marigold showed that a fine-tuned image generator also makes a strong depth estimator.

00:00:43.354 --> 00:00:46.534
But after fine-tuning, it no longer generates images.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.234
Chapter two.

00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:50.943
Depth, written as an image.

00:00:55.558 --> 00:00:56.564
Here's the idea.

00:00:56.564 --> 00:00:59.582
Encode depth as an image, with the same autoencoder.

00:00:59.582 --> 00:01:01.930
Put it in the same token sequence.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.942
And let one transformer denoise both.

00:01:06.175 --> 00:01:08.576
Start with the distance along each pixel's ray.

00:01:09.115 --> 00:01:09.942
Take its log.

00:01:12.055 --> 00:01:16.778
Map this image's own percentile range onto minus one to zero point nine five.

00:01:17.198 --> 00:01:19.856
The sky goes to plus one, with a margin before it.

00:01:20.276 --> 00:01:23.488
Copy it into three channels and encode it like any photo.

00:01:23.488 --> 00:01:26.992
Because the range is set per image, the depth is relative: right

00:01:26.992 --> 00:01:29.619
up to a scale and shift in log space.

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.482
The report's whole architecture.

00:01:32.482 --> 00:01:36.865
A prompt, an image and a depth map, through the backbone's own encoders.

00:01:37.285 --> 00:01:40.799
Their tokens form one sequence, and one transformer denoises it.

00:01:41.219 --> 00:01:43.323
Out come a picture and its depth.

00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:45.126
No depth head, no second network.

00:01:47.008 --> 00:01:49.748
Take a five hundred and twelve pixel square picture.

00:01:50.168 --> 00:01:53.994
The autoencoder makes a thirty-two by thirty-two grid, each cell a

00:01:53.994 --> 00:01:55.385
hundred and twenty-eight channels.

00:01:55.805 --> 00:01:59.803
Each cell is one token: one thousand and twenty-four image tokens.

00:02:00.223 --> 00:02:03.645
The depth map adds another thousand and twenty-four, right after them

00:02:03.645 --> 00:02:04.889
in the same sequence.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.497
Cut the picture and its depth map into patches.

00:02:09.497 --> 00:02:11.051
Each patch becomes one token.

00:02:11.471 --> 00:02:16.090
They join one sequence: the text, then every image token, then every depth token.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.562
How does the model tell them apart?

00:02:18.562 --> 00:02:22.079
First, a depth token has the same row and column as its

00:02:22.079 --> 00:02:25.010
image patch, so attention knows they describe the same point.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.608
Second, a time coordinate the backbone already used to keep reference images

00:02:29.608 --> 00:02:32.046
apart: zero for image, one for depth.

00:02:32.466 --> 00:02:37.723
Third, a learned modality embedding of four rows says which role each token plays.

00:02:37.723 --> 00:02:42.229
It is the only new tensor: twenty-four thousand five hundred and seventy-six

00:02:42.229 --> 00:02:44.857
parameters, on a thirty-two billion parameter model.

00:02:45.277 --> 00:02:48.454
And each grid gets its own noise level, so one can be

00:02:48.454 --> 00:02:50.307
generated while the other is given clean.

00:02:51.983 --> 00:02:55.652
With that sequence, the four tasks differ only in what starts as noise.

00:02:56.072 --> 00:02:57.974
Both noisy: a picture with its depth.

00:02:59.003 --> 00:03:01.523
Photo given clean: only depth is denoised.

00:03:01.943 --> 00:03:04.342
Depth given clean: paint a picture onto it.

00:03:04.762 --> 00:03:07.515
Depth left as noise: a plain image generator.

00:03:08.925 --> 00:03:12.001
Give depth clean, and the picture is painted onto it.

00:03:12.001 --> 00:03:15.077
One Colosseum depth map, a sunken ruin under the sea.

00:03:15.497 --> 00:03:19.007
Then a neon city, a jungle ruin, a castle of sand.

00:03:19.007 --> 00:03:20.283
Four prompts, one geometry.

00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:22.739
Chapter three.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:24.253
Does it beat the specialist?

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:32.712
For the benchmarks, image to depth runs four steps from noise, with

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:33.946
the photo given clean.

00:03:34.366 --> 00:03:35.765
From ten different seeds.

00:03:35.765 --> 00:03:38.212
That is forty transformer passes per picture.

00:03:38.632 --> 00:03:41.285
Each seed is aligned to the first with a scale and

00:03:41.285 --> 00:03:42.250
shift in log space.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:43.995
Then a per-pixel median.

00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:47.969
About fifteen seconds a picture for the big model, where the leading

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:49.955
specialist takes zero point four one.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.768
The headline, on Marigold's five benchmarks: two point nine two against three

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.228
point one two for the leading specialist.

00:03:58.228 --> 00:03:59.282
Lower is better.

00:03:59.702 --> 00:04:01.185
But read panel c.

00:04:01.185 --> 00:04:04.151
Iris-Z only passes the specialist at forty passes.

00:04:04.151 --> 00:04:07.859
At one seed, the SDK's default, it is slightly behind.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.512
The article checked the claim, and found conditions attached.

00:04:12.932 --> 00:04:16.862
On the specialist's own suite, a second metric still goes to the specialist.

00:04:17.282 --> 00:04:21.311
The three point one two baseline is the authors' own run, though

00:04:21.311 --> 00:04:23.660
it reproduces the specialist's published numbers closely.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.292
And the code is Apache, but the weights are under the FLUX non-commercial license.

00:04:29.725 --> 00:04:33.285
A big image generator can learn geometry with almost no new parts.

00:04:33.285 --> 00:04:35.659
As a depth tool, it is still slow.

00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:41.509
Depth as an image, one sequence, one transformer.

00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:42.535
Thanks for watching!

00:04:43.375 --> 00:04:45.581
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:45.581 --> 00:04:46.211
I'm Perpetua.

00:04:46.211 --> 00:04:46.526
Bye!
